nodecode-refusal is a rewrite of 0ldev's 0ldev/refusal:

  https://github.com/0ldev/refusal
  commit b1bd080caeba423309d437f7df6ee564bd5d27dd (2026-11-08)
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 0ldev (LICENSE)

The original is a plugin for another agent harness: it is handed every model
response by that harness's middleware, and when the response declined the
request it rewrites the response's metadata so the harness switches the turn
to the next model in a fallback chain. This rewrite keeps that reading of an
answer and reimplements it in Common Lisp against Nodecode's own seams:
detect.lisp is the pattern tables and the detector (refusal/patterns.py and
refusal/detector.py), reduced to the phrasings that carry the decision, with
the same order of questions -- a deflection, then a partial answer, then an
explicit decline, then an invocation of policy -- and with the technical
inability escape kept. cell.lisp is the plugin's two middlewares as the two
points Nodecode already has: a :FRAME hook that queues a session when a turn
ends, read off the publishing thread, and a :ROUND hook that puts the
operator's preprompt into a request about to be asked.

What was left out, and why: the harness-specific fallback machinery, because
Nodecode's own turn loop already retries on the next candidate when a
provider signals a refusal -- what it cannot see is a model that declines in
prose, which is the whole of what this cell adds, and it asks the same route
again rather than moving the session to another model. The response-shape
handling (OpenAI, Anthropic, Codex payloads and their finish_reason /
stop_reason fields) is not needed, since the cell reads a turn's answer text,
not a provider payload. The per-provider request injection shapes in
preprompt.py collapse to the one seam Nodecode has. The persisted arming
table is not kept: a session's level lives in this process and is forgotten
when the cell stops, which is what the original does by default.
